{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"submission_c14.json\",\"summary\":\"Third candidate (action=obligation) on an authority-transition gap, plus a map extension; preflight-validated with valid=True and no diagnostics.\\n\\nSourced discrepancy: the TimeoutNow receive handler validates nothing beyond the RPC-header version window. TimeoutNowRequest carries only an RPCHeader, so no term is available for a currency check (commands.go:204-218); processRPC applies only the version gate before dispatch (raft.go:1396-1412); and timeoutNow then clears the known leader, sets the node to Candidate and raises candidateFromLeadershipTransfer, responding success unconditionally (raft.go:2208-2214). That flag is the code's own 'abusable' privilege: runCandidate skips the pre-vote campaign and sends RequestVote with LeadershipTransfer=true, which peers grant despite already having a leader (raft.go:284-307 and requestVote). By contrast, requestVote and requestPreVote both reject senders that are not in the receiver's configuration, and leadership votes are granted only when the privilege is set.\\n\\nProduct contents: obligation claim-timeout-now-attribution (implementation_semantics) — a TimeoutNow request is honoured only when its sender is attributed to the receiver's authority context, at minimum a voter of the receiver's latest configuration; one question grounded on the new principal Fact f-leader-transfer-privilege (A2), direct Behavior b-timeout-now (A2), lifecycle establishment, with supporting behaviors b-vote-campaign, b-vote-grant and b-leadership-transfer; and one physical binding (symbol timeoutNow, raft.go:2209-2214).\\n\\nMap extension (base version 7): new Behavior b-timeout-now, new Fact f-leader-transfer-privilege, and a new consumer edge on b-vote-campaign; no accepted object's meaning, identity or existing edges changed, so the two confirmed findings keep their original basis.\\n\\nRetained counterevidence and unknowns, not suppressed: the version window still applies, only a leader normally sends TimeoutNow so the handler may rely on leader discipline, the missing term check may be a design consequence rather than oversight, and the LeadershipTransfer API doc presents mixed-cluster transfers as safe. Reachability by a non-member sender, the effect on a healthy leader, and whether the design intends to trust leader discipline are the explicit premises a later check must resolve. No violation is asserted yet.\"}"}}
